From: "Scott C. Herman" <70400.1036@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 24 Jan 94 22:02:55 EST
Subject: [#94-1-487] Book alert: _Seizing the Future_, by Michael G. Zey

NOTE:  I am borrowing Scott Herman's CompuServe account number to
post messages to the Extropians' list.  Please address your
responses to Mark Plus.

A new book of significance:

	_Seizing the Future: How the Coming Revolution in Science,
Technology, and Industry Will Expand the Frontiers of Human
Potential and Reshape the Planet_, by Michael G. Zey, Ph.D.  New
York: Simon & Schuster, 1994.  ISBN 0-671-74948-X.  US $24.00.

	We know that Extropianism's time is arriving when
individuals spontaneously begin to promote highly extropic
visions of the future.  Last year it was biophysicist Gregory
Stock with his book _Metaman_.  This year it is sociologist
Michael G. Zey with _Seizing the Future_.
	I had a feeling of deja vu when I read Zey's opening
paragraph:
< The human species is about to burst the boundaries of nature
and unleash the power of its technology and human ingenuity,
hurtling itself to the next stage of its evolution.  In doing so,
it will fulfill its destiny to expand its own capabilities and
enhance the planet and the universe.  Humanity is programmed,
perhaps genetically, for growth and progress.  Unlike other
species, humanity cannot and will not stand still. >
	Had Max More written this?  I spoke to Max in person the
other day, showing him my copy of Zey's book, but he was not
familiar with Zey.  After reading _Seizing the Future_, I think
it's fair to describe it as an independent attempt to build a
philosophical-cultural movement similar to Extropianism.  The
blurb says that Dr. Zey, in addition to being a professor of
business administration at Montclair State College in New Jersey,
is also the executive director of something called the
Expansionary Institute.  I've written to Dr. Zey to tell him
about ExI and to ask for information about his organization.  
	Among the high points of his book, Zey:

	(1) provides a theoretical justification for the "imperative
of growth" on pp.17-46, the only long-term alternative being
decline and extinction;	
	(2) advocates on pp. 145-158 the development of medical
nanotechnology for radical life extension, even immortality if
possible, and counters many of the common objections against
immortalism (but seemingly without being aware of cryonics);
	(3) describes the values necessary for maintaining an
"Expansionary Culture" on pp. 287-331, including a commitment to
individual freedom (he's especially critical of the war on
drugs), rationality, a positive view of production and
consumption, hospitality towards productive immigrants, and what
we call Dynamic Optimism;	     
	(4) refutes the claims of radical environmentalists,
including Al Gore, on pp. 354-368.

	Zey also advocates the creation of a new academic
discipline, called "Expansionary Studies," which "would
essentially provide a knowledge of the ideological and technical
underpinnings of growth and progress.  Courses [in the
Expansionary Studies curriculum] would deal with such subjects as
the history of the idea of progress, technological underpinnings
of society, futurology, creative thinking, and social and
institutional foundations of growth."  
	Zey finally calls for the creation of an Expansionary
coalition to counter the well-organized environmentalist and neo-
Malthusian organizations which have exerted increasing control
over the media and the public debate about our future.  Here is
the sort of thing ExI could get into.  I think Dr. Zey could be a
very useful person to try to recruit into Extropianism!    
