From: LEVY%LENNY@venus.cis.yale.edu
Date: Tue, 25 Jan 1994 00:58:44 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-1-490] meat

Tim Goodwin repeats the oft-cited fact that most of the world's grain goes to
feed animals that are in turn eaten, and that many people are starving for want
of grain.  I would point out that starvation is almost always the result of
*politics* (some bureaucrat deciding who gets what), and not of *enonomics*
(scarcity).  For example, it is well known that during the Ethiopian famine
several years ago, large stores of grain were rotting in Ethiopian cities,
because their distribution to poor regions was being blocked by the government
there.  Similar situations caused the starvation in Somalia.

I myself am a vegetarian.  I made the decision after reading (on this List)
the statistics about vegetarians living longer.  Since then, I have come to
think also in terms of the hypocrisy inherent in eating other mammals.  
Consider, for example the cute photos and drawings of cows that are popular
as home decorations.  It strikes me as perverse that the same people who
adore these images can consume the carcasses of such animals.  I see
the emotional double-think required for such behavior as similar in kind
(if not in degree) to the behavior of concentration camp doctors like Mengele,
who would chat and joke with prisoners (and play with the children among them) 
before sending them to the gas chambers.

As I recall, Robert Heinlein considered it hypocritical to eat anything that
you couldn't kill and process yourself.  His solution was to encourage
people to learn how to hunt; mine is not to eat other mammals to being with.

Of course, as a good libertarian, I would not think of preventing others from
eating animals, if that is their choice.

-- Simon!




