From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 25 Jan 94 01:11:04 EST
Subject: [#94-1-491] AI: Theory of Neuronal Attractors

	I have thought for some time that Gerald Edelman needed to read Stuart
Kauffman for including complexity theory into his Theory of Neuronal Group
Selection.  (I think it will be reformulated into a Theory of Neuronal
Attractors).  For those of you who have read "Bright Air, Brilliant Fire" and
"Origins of Order", I thought you would be interested to know that the two were
linked in an article in Science Magazine, Vol. 263, Jan. 14, 1994, pgs. 181-3.
The article was reporting on the "What is Life?" meeting held in Dublin, Ireland
on Sep. 20-22, 1993 to commemorate Erwin Schrodinger's initial lectures on the
subject 50 years ago (where he introduced the concept of "negentropy", the
precursor to today's "extropy").  Manfred Eigen, Stephen Jay Gould and others
add their observations on where we are headed.

	Edelman reported progress on Darwin III, his latest model of perception
machines.  "...Can discriminate between light at the center of its field of
vision and light and stimulation at the periphery.  Through the strengthening
and selection of particular circuits as a result of the experience acquired, the
automaton can track signals from lit objects.  This ability to select groups of
"synapses" makes Darwin III closer to the human brain than any other
computer-based simulation so far attempted.  Edelman felt, however, that
attempts to make a perception machine that is actually conscious, using a Turing
machine-based computer, is bound to fail owing to the dynamic and selective
processes at work in the human brain."

	I am very pleased to see these two linked up as I foresee some very
fruitful possibilities between Kauffman's use of complexity theory to develop
universal rules of biology and his adaptive molecular evolution and Edelman's
TNGS and his Darwin series of AI perception machines.  A very important
development.

